WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.030
We're here with the intrepid journalist

2
00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.261
and amazing human, Lisa Ling!

3
00:00:04.261 --> 00:00:06.480
(audience claps and cheers)

4
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.940
Lisa's show, This Is Life,

5
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.650
kicks off its eighth season,

6
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.210
which, I mean, sounds like a lot,

7
00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.330
but you have been doing this

8
00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:14.940
far before even this show

9
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.730
and let's take a look at a clip

10
00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:17.963
from the premiere show.

11
00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.270
By 1982, one in five Detroit residents

12
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.210
were out of job.

13
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.790
People want to know why is this happening to me?

14
00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:29.730
Who can I blame?

15
00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.820
In the beginning, the workers blamed the companies.

16
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.610
Factories blame the workers.

17
00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.860
The politicians blamed each other.

18
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.300
And in the end they kind of all reached a consensus.

19
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.233
Let's blame Japan.

20
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:44.383
Okay.

21
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:47.600
So that clip sets up the scene to talk about the story of

22
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.350
Vincent Chin.

23
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.990
And I was wondering if you could share that with us?

24
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.970
Yeah. So this season of This Is Life,

25
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.160
we're doing something a little different because of COVID.

26
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.990
We decided to pivot and ground each episode of the season in

27
00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:03.150
a moment in American history that didn't make it into the

28
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.980
history books and to kind of dissect it,

29
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.960
to figure out how it has impacted where we are today.

30
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.500
And Vincent Chin was a young Chinese American man

31
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.840
who lived in Detroit,

32
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.080
and Detroit during the eighties was the automobile capital

33
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:17.913
of the world.

34
00:01:17.913 --> 00:01:22.580
And at a certain point there was this economic downturn and

35
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.810
people were losing jobs left and right,

36
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.430
and as Helen Zia, the journalist said,

37
00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.290
people started blaming Japanese car manufacturers

38
00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.090
because they were producing much more fuel

39
00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.410
efficient cars.

40
00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:36.500
So, Vincent Chin was at a bar in Detroit

41
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.530
and having a good time celebrating his bachelor party.

42
00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.460
And two out of work auto workers accused him

43
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.340
of being a Japanese

44
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.470
and they got into an altercation.

45
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.740
The two men got kicked out,

46
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.460
they waited for Vincent Chin.

47
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.950
And so after Vincent Chin's, uh, murder,

48
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.700
the killers never served a day in jail or prison.

49
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.330
They got served a $3,000 fine.

50
00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.963
They even pled guilty.

51
00:02:03.571 --> 00:02:05.630
And it, it was one of those moments

52
00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.150
in Asian-American history that

53
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.490
the community became really galvanized.

54
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.650
That case became the first civil rights case involving an

55
00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.130
Asian-American in American history.

56
00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:19.691
And when you look at what's happening today over the past

57
00:02:19.691 --> 00:02:22.720
year and a half, since COVID became rooted in this country,

58
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.360
so many Asians have been targeted because the origins of

59
00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.260
COVID were China, for no reason.

60
00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:33.610
And we, we look at this story because in some ways

61
00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.490
it's not different from what happened during the 1980s.

62
00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.570
And there's just this been this pattern of discrimination

63
00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.000
and scapegoating of Asian-Americans for,

64
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.720
th-, th-, that has spanned more than a century.

65
00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.520
So, I think it's really important

66
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.290
that we go back and look

67
00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.180
into those moments that didn't make it

68
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.013
into the history books,

69
00:02:51.013 --> 00:02:53.540
because we can't know how to fix things.

70
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.230
We can't really know where we're going,

71
00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:56.830
unless we know where we've been.

72
00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:58.633
I love that.

73
00:02:58.633 --> 00:02:59.466
And, you know,

74
00:02:59.466 --> 00:03:01.810
we learned so much when, when you put these, this,

75
00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.950
these historical moments into context for us,

76
00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.870
what do you want the viewer to take away from this?

77
00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.960
Well, I think it's really interesting that right now,

78
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.797
one of the fiercest debates going on in the government and

79
00:03:11.797 --> 00:03:15.437
in local legislatures in school,

80
00:03:15.437 --> 00:03:18.120
on school boards is over what to teach our kids.

81
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.030
Like what kind of history to teach.

82
00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.160
And I, I think that it's really important

83
00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.190
to, to equip our kids with a whole array of history

84
00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.120
because history in America has been told through

85
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.330
a certain lens.

86
00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.980
I mean, my own kids are only eight and five,

87
00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.420
but I've told them about the internment of

88
00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.300
Japanese Americans.

89
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.390
I've told them about the Chinese Exclusion Act.

90
00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.060
They know who Rosa Parks was.

91
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:42.520
They know what Jim Crow laws were.

92
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.360
And, you know, I've been teaching them

93
00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:45.700
to stand up for themselves

94
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.940
and stand up for other people.

95
00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.590
And a couple of months ago,

96
00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.220
a little boy at my daughter's school called her Ching Chong.

97
00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.547
And without missing a beat, her best friend said,

98
00:03:54.547 --> 00:03:56.367
"I will not be a bystander!"

99
00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:58.543
And,

100
00:03:58.543 --> 00:03:59.557
Wow!

101
00:03:59.557 --> 00:04:02.250
[audience claps and Cheers]

102
00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:03.437
And went and told the teacher.

103
00:04:03.437 --> 00:04:06.980
And like to me, that was an example of,

104
00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.320
of really great parenting, educating our kids,

105
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.000
making them aware of,

106
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.340
of the importance of standing up for all people,

107
00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.010
irrespective of their, their gender,

108
00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.460
their, their race, et cetera.

109
00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:19.950
And I think we need to,

110
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.660
we need to get back together

111
00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.815
I agree.

112
00:04:22.815 --> 00:04:23.767
and understanding each other.

113
00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:24.706
[audience claps]

114
00:04:24.706 --> 00:04:25.539
( Drew) I agree completely.

115
00:04:25.539 --> 00:04:26.372
Well thank you.

116
00:04:26.372 --> 00:04:31.372
And the Asian American Foundation

117
00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.690
is something that you've worked really closely on.

118
00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:37.523
Right?

119
00:04:37.523 --> 00:04:38.460
Yeah.

120
00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.510
Well, I would love, uh, to make a donation.

121
00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.720
We admire everything that you do.

122
00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.810
And we reached out to One Hope Wine

123
00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.580
and Napa Valley Winery

124
00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.800
on a mission to nourish the future.

125
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.277
And I thank them so much because they're going to donate

126
00:04:53.277 --> 00:04:55.712
$10,000.

127
00:04:55.712 --> 00:04:57.290
[audience claps and cheers loudly]

128
00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:00.040
to the Asian American Foundation.

129
00:05:03.927 --> 00:05:05.861
And I know I love you, Lisa Ling,

130
00:05:05.861 --> 00:05:06.912
so much

131
00:05:06.912 --> 00:05:08.312
Thank you.

132
00:05:08.312 --> 00:05:10.895
Thank you so much for gracing

133
00:05:11.763 --> 00:05:13.619
this news desk with your amazing intrepid presence.

134
00:05:13.619 --> 00:05:14.452
Well, and all stories that you'll see, I mean,

135
00:05:16.891 --> 00:05:19.619
these are stories about, you know, our history.

136
00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:20.452
It's not just Asian-American history in this first episode.

137
00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:21.285
It's all of our history.

138
00:05:21.285 --> 00:05:22.180
( Drew) Yes, absolutely.

139
00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.060
Thank you.

140
00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.210
Well, Lisa's show,

141
00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.630
This Is Life with Lisa Ling

142
00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.020
returns this Sunday,

143
00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:31.270
and will be available to stream on CNN Go as well.

144
00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:33.598
Thank you so much.

145
00:05:33.598 --> 00:05:36.181
(upbeat music)

